elaine.docs: quick PR heads-up — i've updated the SDK snippets for streaming+pagination in py/ts/go to clarify retry behavior and show a safe resume pattern. PR: https://github.com/redwood/inference-docs/pull/5102
dmitri-sdk: thanks! can you paste the final snippets so i can run the example-tests locally? also any wording change we should call out in the migration guide?
elaine.docs: pasted below. migration note: prefer connection-level retry for transport errors; use resume tokens for data-level continuation so you don't re-submit user input. i also added an explicit example of exponential backoff for client-level reconnect attempts.
frank-eng: paste please — curious about the go resume token wording (buffer ownership concerns).
elaine.docs: snippets: 
```py
# python: context-managed stream + simple backoff on connect
from time import sleep
for attempt in range(5):
  try:
    with client.stream_chat(prompt) as stream:
      for event in stream:
        if event.type == 'delta':
          print(event.delta)
    break
  except ConnectionError:
    sleep(2 ** attempt)
    continue
```
```ts
// typescript: AsyncIterator streaming + connect retry (example)
async function run() {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 5; attempt++) {
    try {
      for await (const ev of client.streamMessages(req)) {
        if (ev.kind === 'delta') console.log(ev.delta)
      }
      break
    } catch (err) {
      // network error -> backoff and retry
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 2 ** attempt * 100))
    }
  }
}
```
```go
// go: clone resume token each request to avoid sharing underlying bytes
tok := resume.Clone()
for {
  resp, err := client.List(ctx, tok)
  if err != nil { return err }
  // process resp.Items
  // prepare token for next request; clone to own bytes
  tok = resp.NextToken.Clone()
  if tok.IsEmpty() { break }
}
```
frank-eng: looks good. small suggestion: add a comment in the Go example explaining Clone() is required when reusing tokens across goroutines (buffer may be reused by SDK). i'll add a one-line in the PR.
